Z||000000||Xtreme Fiero's||12-24-2000||11:38 PM||||<BR>My mom & I are argueing (SP) the <B>dependabilty</B> (SP) of a Fiero. She wants to know who drives/ Depends on thier Fiero on a daily basis. She says that driving a Fiero is only an Expensive (SP) hobby and that they can't get you to & from work daily. <P>Please respond as I will make her read this.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Slick781||12-24-2000||11:45 PM||||I drive my dad's 87 coupe to school, and everywhere else i go every day... i think it now has like 180,000+ miles on it... havent had a problem with since we did some maintainence to it about 6 or 7 months ago, maybe longer<P>David||||reg||1
Z||000002||WisconsinGT||12-25-2000||12:00 AM||||My 88GT was a daily driver until I heard a loud knock coming from the engine the other day. I guess it is time for a rebuild. Anyone know how much I should pay to have someone drop the old engine and put in a new/rebuilt one? 	Thanks!||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroMaster88||12-25-2000||12:07 AM||||I drive my car everyday.....More dependable than my friends 98 Mitsu Eclipse!   It only has 30,000 miles on it, but it starts right up, no problems, even when it is -20 out!   Gets about 36 MPG too!<P>Good luck,<P>James||||reg||1
Z||000004||Xtreme Fiero's||12-25-2000||12:34 AM||||WisconsinGT: "Thank you so much for your wonderful input"<P>Thanks,<BR>   Tom's MOM||||reg||1
Z||000005||mrfiero||12-25-2000||12:38 AM||||I have driven Fieros almost exclusively for about 11 years now.  My only "lapse" was when I bought a new S-10 back in '94.....I still had a Fiero, so  wasn't without.<P>My first Fiero was purchased in April 1990 and I drove it until December 1995, when it was totaled in a wreck (I had 115K miles on her).  I have since owned and driven over 2 dozen Fieros.  Combined mileage from all of them totals over 300,000 mostly trouble free miles.  <P>I have only had one motor let go on me and it was on my first one at 103K miles.  I have owned several low mileage Fieros and many high mileage ones too.....all were very reliable on a daily basis.  Just regular basic maintenance is all I've had to do to any of them.<P>That's why I cringe when people deem the Fiero as "high maintenance" or "unreliable".  It simply isn't true.||||reg||10
Z||000006||Pontiaddict||12-25-2000||01:08 AM||||My Fiero is my only running car. I've put alot of money into it, but it's more of a upgrade thing than broken parts.<BR> <BR>As for other experiences.<P>My dads '89 s10 4x4...<BR>new motor<BR>new halshafts<BR>new brakes (rear wheel cylinder died about 10 times)<BR>new bushings<BR>some exhaust work<BR>and that's just the things I remember.<P>My '79 Chevy Van<BR>Needs brakes<BR>needs cooling system<BR>that would explain the dropped valve<P>My old '86 Trans am<BR>bad brakes<BR>bad throwout bearing<BR>the computer died (Computer controlled carb)<BR>Half the seatbelts were broken (recalled)<BR>Electrical problems galore||||reg||1
Z||000007||kslish||12-25-2000||01:11 AM||||I have been driving my GT everyday to work this winter ever since my winter car's (an '89 Olds Cutlass Supreme coupe) fuel pump decided it didn't like to run in sub-freezing temperatures.  The only reason I don't like driving it in the winter is because of snow and subjecting my Fieros to road salt kills me.  I have become a master of driving the Fiero in the snow though.<P>When I was in college, my '86 SC was my daily transportation for three years.<P>As always, routine maintainence is key.  <P>Ken S.<BR>'86 SC and '87 GT||||reg||1
Z||000008||JetroGT||12-25-2000||01:13 AM||||Fieros are great for commuting. Thats what they were origionally designed for. After I fixed all of the neglect from the previous owners, it runs great. I used to drive it every day to and from work, a 70 mile round trip. The only problem I had was that the starter went out, and that could happen to any car. As long as you take care of your car, it will take care of you.<P>86 se auto  black<BR>87 GT 5spd  blue||||reg||14
Z||000009||2xsess||12-25-2000||02:13 AM||||I drive mine everyday. All 5 of min ehave been daily drivers. None of them have been low mileage cars either. Never had any serious problems, just routine maintenance.<P><BR>Hi Tom's Mom  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P><BR><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/My_car_(large)sig_1.JPG"> ||||reg||14
Z||000010||Standard||12-25-2000||02:34 AM||||I've been driving my GT just about every day since I got it last February.  Put about 1,200 miles on it a month.  Hasn't let out on a trip yet.  It's not quite perfect, but with 153,000 miles it's still started every freakin' cold day we've had up here and has survived -15* nights.. oil pressure is a little low, but it's still within specs.  It's far from perfect but it runs.  And I'm getting an average of 20 mpg.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Motorhead||12-25-2000||02:59 AM||||My 86 coupe is the daily driver. Great MPG and lots of stares from Jap car commuters! Guess they wish they weren't so boring!!!  148,000 miles, A quart of oil every 3,000 miles with changes every 6,000 miles. You can eat off the engine,"CLEAN"!<BR>God, I love my car!!<P>Mike<P>------------------<BR> bluewater46@hotmail.com<BR>1986 coupe<BR>1984 coupe<BR> <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1372350&a=10592337&p=35451888"> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||Motorhead||12-25-2000||03:04 AM||||Did I mention my third Fiero arrives this Friday? <BR>Another 86!!! And RED to boot!<P>------------------<BR> bluewater46@hotmail.com<BR>1986 coupe<BR>1984 coupe<BR> <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1372350&a=10592337&p=35451888"> ||||reg||1
Z||000013||Cheever2||12-25-2000||06:37 AM||||Been driving mine everyday for a year.  There has been maintenance, yes, but there's nothing else I'd rather drive that is this affordable.  <BR>-<BR>Fieros are still a bargain, but they may not be for long.<BR>-<BR>The real cost issue is when you gotta have ANOTHER one, then another...<BR>(just cause you WANT them, not as replacements)<BR> ||||reg||6
Z||000014||firstfiero||12-25-2000||08:22 AM||||       I drive mine everyday! and your right get them now  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> because they won't be cheap for long.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> Their an american original! 86se||||reg||1
Z||000015||JohnnyK||12-25-2000||10:34 AM||||Sorry man, they will be cheap forever...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000016||William Federle||12-25-2000||11:00 AM||||I live in Milwaukee and work in Fond du Lac (a 50 mile drive each way) and I drive my 1984 Fiero SE every day. I got it for $500.00 and I've had to stick about $1000.00 into it (including $400.00 for 4 tires and wheels after the original ones were ripped off). I put about 30000 miles on it this past year. I have to fix something from time to time, but except for some starter, coolant-leak, and battery problems, it's been pretty reliable. I do have an AAA card though because if it does break down on the road, I could be a pretty long walk from help. It's still cheaper than car payments, and sometimes the drive is the best part of the day.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by William Federle (edited 12-25-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000017||v8vinnie||12-25-2000||01:13 PM||||hey dude, I drive mine almost every day or im on my v-starr 1100, i dont car about how many miles or if it is stock or modified. IN my opinion, people are more imporant than cars ,enjoy your ride,i should know,I was in a corvette club for 10 years and im fedup with whodoesent drive thier cars 50 milesa month and so on.||||reg||1
Z||000018||ChopTop||12-25-2000||02:20 PM||||My wife and I picked up our first Fiero in 88 (85GT), we still own it but it's now a summer only car.  I picked her up a 88 4 cylinder auto coupe in 91, it's still her daily driver, that has about 120,000 on it.  The only major repair was the plastic timing gear that let go around 80,000.  The reason I don't want her driving it presently is because we have over 30 inches of snow and it needs new tires, plus some of the snow banks are taller than the car, so it's more a safety concern than a reliability one.  But once we get new tires on it shortly I'm sure she will want to begin driving it again (she doesn't car to drive our Jeep because its a manual).    The last Fiero we picked up was in 94, drove it for a couple years and now that one is also a fair weather car, and hopefully a show car someday. The only major problems I've had with my 85GT and 85SE has been the TCC (transmission solenoid) has gone out on both cars.<BR>Your best bet is to purchase a car that you know has been well maintained, that doesn't mean it won't let you down, after all these cars are going on 17 years old.  This list and Fiero clubs are an excellent resource to help you both locate and help maintain a Feiro.  Overall, we've been happy with our Fiero experience.||||reg||1
Z||000019||lou_dias||12-25-2000||02:26 PM||||Since I got my rebuilt 3.4 in from the Fiero Factory, the odo read 151k on May 27, 2000, now I'm at 170k with no engine problems.  that's like 3k miles a month...||||reg||1
Z||000020||Philphine||12-25-2000||03:55 PM||||my '87 is my main car. had some clutch and engine work done but it's a car and that's expected. had it for close to 4 years and have bought 3 more since. <P>you'd be right in assuming we're a biased group mrs...uh...tom's mom, but thats going to happen with any car you're for or against. i'll say it's as good as any gm car of it's era for what that may be worth to you. <P>don't just pick out wisconsin gt's input to justify your rejection of fieros. plus notice that even wth a fairly serious engine problem he still wants to repair the car. he didn't mention getting rid or it. thats the second thing, they really are neat little cars. keepers. <P>while you may not ever be fully enthused by tom getting a fiero, i don't think you'll come to regret it.<P>please mrs. tom's mom, let tom come out and play with fieros. <P>------------------<BR>Phil T<P>'87 coupe (feral)<BR>'84 indy<BR>'84 coupe (cheap thrill)||||reg||1
Z||000021||rogergarrison||12-25-2000||04:27 PM||||I drive my kitcar Fiero everyday except on ice n snow. Prob about 200 miles a week, 500 or more a week in summer<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1234884&a=9116379&p=35934618"> ||||reg||1
Z||000022||WisconsinGT||12-25-2000||05:25 PM||||As Philphine said,"even wth a fairly serious engine problem he still wants to repair the car. he didn't mention getting rid or it."<P>That is so true! I will never get rid of my Fiero. I have known for quite some time that I needed a rebuild. I think you have to realize that any 12 year old vehicle will need some work. If you want a dependable car buy a new one. If you want a soild dependable used car then get what you feel is a solid car, and fix it up. That way you know that what you have worked on is new and solid. Good luck!||||reg||1
Z||000023||Greg Piet||12-25-2000||06:21 PM||||Mine is a daily driver, which also participates in autocross and rallycross.  I have had my share of mechanical failures though, which is expected in a 15 yr old car.<BR>This last summer drove from one ocean to the other and back...  one way towing a VW Dune buggy.<P>one problem during that entire trek...  Clutch slave cylinder gave out.||||reg||1
Z||000024||Uaana||12-25-2000||06:36 PM||||Yep drive it every day!<BR>Winter, summer all points inbetween.<BR>Never had a problem except for the EGR valve and that was a $30 fix||||reg||1
Z||000025||Will||12-25-2000||07:36 PM||||"Routine Maintenance" is mentioned several times in this thread. What some seem to overlook is that EVERY car needs maintenance, even Rolls-Royces. If you buy a car that has been well maintained, it will probably be problem free. If you buy one that has been poorly maintained, then you will have to put money into it to bring it up to spec, but once that is done it will be reliable. I have had to replace all four front ball joints on both of my cars because the prvious owners did not bother, even when the odomoters ticked over 100K. For the record, my first car has 153K miles (~135K engine miles), and my second has 117K. I picked up the first in non-running condition for $400 and dropped in a used engine. I picked up the second for $700; aside from ball joints, it only required nickel and dime maintenance.<BR>Both are V6's and plenty of fun to drive.<p>[This message has been edited by Will (edited 12-25-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000026||mbramble||12-25-2000||07:47 PM||||Xtreme, <P>My daughter bought an '87 Fiero in '94.  She drove it until she got married and sold it to her brother.  He drove it until he went in the Navy and didn't want to take it to NY.  None of them really took too good of care of it.  My son "gave" it to me in lieu of some money he owed me and it ran like crap when I got it.  Spent $150 at the Pontiac dealer to replace a bad coil pack and to decarb the engine.  It has been purring like a kitten for a year and now my youngest son drives it everyday.<P>If the car is in good shape and properly maintained, it should be plenty reliable.  The expensive part is all the things you want to do to it to make it better!!  I've spent far more on adding power windows/locks/mirrors/trunk, sunroof, etc., than I did to get it running properly and reliably.  None of those expenses were necessary, just nice to have.  But, it's the same kind of thing that a person might do with any car that they consider to be more than just transportation.<P>I'd say that any car that has been driven regularly by three teenagers over a 6 year period with probably $800 total in repair bills is pretty reliable!!  It wasn't until I got ahold of it that it has become an expensive hobby!!  And the $500 or so I have put into it for the extras I wanted has made it a completely different car.  <p>[This message has been edited by mbramble (edited 12-25-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000027||Stoic||12-25-2000||07:50 PM||||I drive my '85 SE to work and back every day.  About 40 minutes one way.  When you are driving an older car, you must expect the odd repair.  I drive mine everywhere, even during winter.   <P>------------------<BR>If you can read this, I'm parked.||||reg||6
Z||000028||80's BOY||12-25-2000||08:30 PM||||hey rogergarrison, i'll bet Tom's mom really appreciates that pic of the girl on the hood  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> now she'll probably never let him on here again!! HA!!! ||||reg||1
Z||000029||Hank is Here||12-25-2000||09:35 PM||||My GT is a daily driver during good weather.  I have only had my GT for 8 months or so, but this summer I was driving it 600 miles plus a week and the cost well...how much is a gallon of gas?  My other 88 coupe which I have had for a year, been in my family for about 6 years and saw minimal maintiance has only needed a water pump and parking brake work...less than $500 in not normal maitiance over 6 years isn't bad.    <BR>If you want a high maintiance vehicle try jeeps... I think with my 66 CJ5 my work time on it is more than driving time, but I love driving that ol' thing.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000030||Formula||12-25-2000||10:17 PM||||160,000 miles.....<P>no major problems, just a nickel here and a nickel there....<P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FormulaSignature2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000031||BAM-BAM||12-25-2000||10:33 PM||||  I drive the silver bullet everyday, even when I'm in the middle of modifications. Drove it for two weeks with nothing on the inside but the gauge pod and the driverseat. Very durable ride.<P> Peace, B2 OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Don B. <BR>85GT<BR>Eibach/KYB Susp.<BR>Mr.Mikes 500 series seats,headliner! MOMO Commando Steering Wheel, White Face Race Style Gauges And Soon To Be More!!! YEAH BABY-YEAH||||reg||1
Z||000032||2birds||12-25-2000||11:02 PM||||Mine's been all over the country (12 states to go, and one requires shipping).  I'd make a road trip with no problem now.  It was our primary vehicle until we had rugrats six years ago.  Almost 13 years and 168,000 miles (150,000 by me), original 2.8, just breaking in its fourth clutch, but I beat it pretty hard. Good thing Getrags are pretty durable.  Replaced all of the balljoints and bushings 5 years ago for no particular reason; just bored, I guess.  2 Oil pressure sending units ($30), a water pump twice (PITA but cheap), an alternator ($80), a clutch slave cylinder ($60) and clutch master cylinder rebuild kit ($15).  Everything else, such as tires,  tuneups, oil changes, radiator flush, same as any other car.  Clutch is no more expensive or difficult than an Escort or Cavalier.  Only thing that's really diferent is insurance stinks when you're young, just a lot more if it's considered a performance car.    ||||reg||1
Z||000033||RaeQwan||12-26-2000||12:43 AM||||drive mine everyday, have had very few problems, all my fault. 135k miles and running on its second engine, the engine itself was out of a 87 with 92k miles on it I think, I've put about 12k miles on the engine and she still runs as sweet and better than alot of other cars. I'll not get rid of my Fiero..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/raeqwanegg.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000034||sdm1964||12-26-2000||06:38 AM||||I have an 85 GT and drive it eveyday, A 50 mile round trip. Other than regular maintenance items I've had to put on a water pump. I've spent more on upgrades than repair parts. I've owned prob 40 different automobiles, Classics to street rods. It is the best of both worlds, not a true power house like a camaro and more style and fun than a toyota tercel. Being in the military reliability is crutial for me. And i would not hesitate in letting any of my 4 daughters own one. With this group of fine folks on the board, parts and advice is only a click away. Come on mom let him come out and play.||||reg||14
Z||000035||BBBAD GT||12-26-2000||09:17 AM||||My 87 GT has been driven daily since I got it back in Jan.  No problems, even with the blizzard we have been getting lately.<P><P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/BBBAD_GT_SIG.gif">    <P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/V8Fiero" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD V8</A><P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/Fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD GT</A><P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD 5.0</A>||||reg||1
Z||000036||ChadMan||12-26-2000||09:19 AM||||From 3/92 to 4/96 I drove an 84 Coupe daily - from 4/96 - present I've driven an 88 Coupe.  The 84 was mechanically reliable, but had a lot of electrical problems and left me stranded several times.  The 88 has been incredibly reliable and has never let me down - not once in almost five years and 50K miles!||||reg||1
Z||000037||MrPBody||12-26-2000||09:19 AM||||<B>Any</B> car can be a pain in the neck.  As 15-year-old cars go, my Fiero ('85 SE 2.5) is relatively reliable; also, it's usually easy and inexpensive to repair.  I do most repairs myself, and drive the car to work every day.  And it's fun to drive!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000038||fierosound||12-26-2000||03:04 PM||||I drive my 1987 GT daily year-round since I bought it in 1991.  Here in Calgary, we can get some extremely cold days in winter, as well as some very heavy snowfall.  I've only gotten stuck in snow once, heavy wet snow about 14 inches deep.  With good all-season, or winter tires, I don't have any qualms about winter driving...<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/calgaryfieros/tonys87.htm" TARGET=_blank>www.geocities.com/calgaryfieros/tonys87.htm</A> ||||reg||10
